course: physics
lesson: 1
title: Why things fall: the apple and the pull of the Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everything that has mass pulls on everything else, and this pull is called gravity.
explain: The Earth is so huge that its pull is the one we feel: it tugs every apple, ball and child towards its middle.
explain: Drop an apple and it does not just fall, it speeds up all the way down, because the Earth keeps pulling the whole time.
explain: Drop a feather and a stone together and the stone wins, but not because it is heavier: the air pushes back on the fluffy feather much more.
explain: On the Moon, where there is no air, an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather, and they landed at exactly the same moment.
fact: The Earth pulls everything towards its middle, so "down" means towards the centre of the Earth wherever you stand.
fact: A falling apple speeds up all the way down because gravity keeps pulling it.
fact: The apple pulls the Earth too, but the Earth is so heavy that it moves far less than the width of an atom.
fact: A ball thrown straight up is pulled by gravity the whole time, even at the top where it seems to stop for a moment.
fact: Something thrown up at about 11 kilometres every second would escape the Earth and never come back.
fact: The higher you jump from, the faster you are going when you land, and bending your knees makes the stop gentler.
fact: People in Australia do not fall off, because there is no real top or bottom to the Earth.
fact: Air pushes back on falling things, and it slows light, fluffy or flat things far more than heavy, compact ones.
fact: A flat sheet of paper flutters down slowly, but the same sheet screwed into a ball drops fast, because only its shape changed.
fact: Without air, a heavy thing and a light thing fall at exactly the same speed: the heavy thing is pulled harder, but it is also harder to get moving, and the two cancel out.
fact: A sheet of paper laid flat on top of a book falls with the book, because the book shields it from the air.
fact: In 1971 the astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a falcon feather on the Moon, and they landed together.
fact: Galileo worked this out nearly 400 years earlier, with careful thinking and experiments with balls rolling down slopes.
fact: A raindrop speeds up until the push of the air equals its weight, and then it falls at a steady speed, so it does not hurt; without air, a drop from a high cloud would arrive faster than a racing car.
exercise: Why does an apple fall to the ground? | The Earth pulls it towards its middle | The air above pushes it down to the ground | The tree lets go of it and it sinks down | Heavy things always want to go low down
exercise: What happens to an apple's speed as it falls? | It speeds up all the way down | It stays the same all the way | It slows down near the ground | It goes fast first, then slower
exercise: Does the falling apple pull on the Earth too? | Yes, but the Earth moves far too little to notice | No, only big things can pull | No, gravity only pulls downwards | Yes, and the Earth jumps up a little bit to meet it
exercise: A ball is thrown straight up. At the very top, is gravity still pulling it? | Yes, gravity pulls it the whole time | No, gravity stops while it is still | No, gravity only works on falling things | Only if the ball is heavy enough
exercise: Why does a ball thrown up come back down? | Gravity takes away its upward speed and pulls it back | The air above it pushes it back down towards the ground | It runs out of push and then drops | The sky is like a lid it bounces off
exercise: Why do gymnasts bend their knees when they land? | It makes the stop slower and gentler | It makes them land faster and much lower | It makes gravity pull on them less hard | It makes them lighter for a short moment
exercise: Which way is "down" for a child in Australia? | Towards the middle of the Earth | Towards the North Pole | Up into the sky, away from Earth | Towards the bottom of the map
exercise: A feather and a stone are dropped together. Why does the stone land first? | The air slows the feather much more | Gravity does not pull feathers | Heavy things are pulled faster always | The feather is pushed up by gravity
exercise: Why did the hammer and the feather land together on the Moon? | There is no air on the Moon to slow the feather | The Moon pulls feathers harder than hammers | The feather was wet and heavy | The Moon has no gravity at all
exercise: Who worked out that heavy and light things fall together, hundreds of years ago? | Galileo | Archimedes | Napoleon | Christopher Columbus
exercise: You screw a flat sheet of paper into a ball. Why does it now fall faster? | Its shape catches less air | It has become much heavier | Gravity likes round things more | The paper has lost its air inside
exercise: A big book and a small book are dropped side by side. What happens? | They hit the floor together | The big book lands far ahead | The small book lands far ahead | The big book falls up first
exercise: How would you test whether heavy things fall faster? | Drop a heavy and a light ball of the same size together | Drop one ball and listen to it | Weigh the balls and then guess | Throw the heavy one down harder and see which one wins the race
exercise: Why don't raindrops hurt like bullets? | Air slows them to a steady, gentle speed | Clouds are very close to the ground anyway | Rain is lighter than the air all around it | Raindrops do not really have any weight at all
exercise: In a room with no air at all, what would a feather and a stone do? | Land at exactly the same moment | The feather would float in the air | The stone would land much first | Both would float and not fall
exercise: If gravity pulls the big book harder, why doesn't it win the race? | It is also harder to get moving, and the two cancel | The big book is pushed by more air | Gravity pulls all books the same | The small book is helped along by the wind as it falls
test: my brother says heavy stuff always falls faster, end of. right? | No, without air heavy and light fall together | Yes, twice as heavy means twice as fast | Yes, that is what Galileo showed | No, light things always fall faster
test: In a cartoon a coyote runs off a cliff and only falls when he looks down. What is wrong? | Gravity pulls him the moment he leaves the edge | Nothing, gravity waits until you notice | He should float up instead | Cartoons are right; looking down adds weight
test: A sheet of paper is laid flat on top of a big book, and both are dropped. What happens? | The paper falls with the book, shielded from the air | The paper flies off and flutters | The paper lands first | The book stops and the paper goes on
test: What would happen if you dug a hole right through the Earth and climbed in? | Once past the middle, "down" would turn into "up" | You would fall out the other side into space | You would float in the hole from the start | You would get heavier and heavier all the way
test: Mate says rain should kill ya, it falls from a mile up. what do you say? | Air slows each drop to a steady speed | Rain is too light for gravity | Clouds throw rain very gently | Raindrops slow down as they get near us
test: Without air, how would a raindrop from a high cloud arrive? | Faster than a racing car | Just as gently as now | It would never reach the ground | It would float back to the cloud
test: Two balls the same size, one steel and one plastic, are dropped from a window. Which is closest to true? | They land at almost the same time | The steel one lands twice as soon | The plastic one does not fall | The plastic one lands first
test: In a game, a heavy sword falls fast and a light coin floats down slowly like a leaf. Is that real? | No, a coin is compact and falls fast too | Yes, coins are too light to fall very fast | Yes, small metal coins are pulled on much less | No, the coin should really float upwards instead
test: Why does a higher wall mean a harder landing? | The longer you fall, the faster you are going | The ground is harder under high walls | Gravity is stronger high up, so it pulls you harder | You get heavier as you fall
test: shut up about gravity, the ball stops at the top cos nothing's pulling it. true? | No, gravity pulls it at the top too | Yes, the pull switches off for a moment | Yes, the air holds it up there | No, the throw is still pulling it up
test: How fast would you need to throw a ball so it never came back? | About 11 kilometres every second | About 100 kilometres every single hour | As fast as a racing car on a track | No speed at all could ever manage it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a feather descends more slowly than a stone when both are released simultaneously in the open air. | The resistance of the air acts more strongly upon the feather | The gravitational attraction upon feathers is negligible | Heavier bodies are invariably accelerated more rapidly | The feather is repelled by the surface of the Earth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the motion of bodies released in the absence of air? | All bodies descend at an identical rate irrespective of weight | Heavier bodies descend at a proportionally greater rate | Lighter bodies remain suspended indefinitely | Bodies descend at a constant speed from release
exam: It is given that a ball is projected vertically upwards. Ascertain the condition of the gravitational force at the highest point of its path. | It continues to act upon the ball | It ceases entirely for an instant | It is reversed and acts upwards | It is replaced by the resistance of the air
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the direction designated "down" upon the Earth. | It is directed towards the centre of the Earth at every location | It is directed towards the South Pole at every location | It is directed towards the North Pole at every location | It is identical in absolute direction at every location
